WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.205 --> 00:00:01.160
- Good morning!

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:04.727
It's time for you to see a Day
in my Life, Weekend Edition.

00:00:04.727 --> 00:00:05.766
Look how I start my day.

00:00:05.766 --> 00:00:06.599
You ready?

00:00:06.599 --> 00:00:07.932
Newf, come here.

00:00:09.177 --> 00:00:11.460
He's literally slapping
me with this tongue.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.805
Bear, can you lay down?

00:00:15.031 --> 00:00:16.781
Ugh, my hair's crazy.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:19.535
(gargling)

00:00:19.535 --> 00:00:23.242
Always brush your teeth
before you eat citrusy foods.

00:00:23.242 --> 00:00:24.952
Fun fact, protects your enamel.

00:00:24.952 --> 00:00:27.929
(gentle music)

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:30.635
Who's ready to make some
orange ricotta almond toast?

00:00:30.635 --> 00:00:31.654
I know I am.

00:00:31.654 --> 00:00:34.375
We have the navel orange
with a classic navel on it.

00:00:34.375 --> 00:00:35.986
We have the Cara Cara orange.

00:00:35.986 --> 00:00:38.162
Cara Cara on the right, navel on the left.

00:00:38.162 --> 00:00:40.988
Toast bread in toaster,
or however you prefer.

00:00:40.988 --> 00:00:42.975
Are there other ways to toast bread?

00:00:42.975 --> 00:00:46.690
(blowtorch whooshing)

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:49.661
The more you can get these
important vital nutrients

00:00:49.661 --> 00:00:51.404
from whole foods, the better,

00:00:51.404 --> 00:00:53.443
and an orange is a great way to do that.

00:00:53.443 --> 00:00:56.576
(upbeat music)
♪ I've been hypnotized by you ♪

00:00:56.576 --> 00:00:58.255
Quarter inch slices on this.

00:00:58.255 --> 00:00:59.357
God, these smell so good.

00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:00.621
The citrus just pops.

00:01:00.621 --> 00:01:01.864
I'm not even terrible at this.

00:01:01.864 --> 00:01:02.697
Oof!

00:01:02.697 --> 00:01:04.132
The beauty that is oranges.

00:01:04.132 --> 00:01:05.681
All right, our toast is done.

00:01:05.681 --> 00:01:07.167
Almond ricotta, check.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.092
You know, I love honey.

00:01:08.092 --> 00:01:08.947
Bear, do you love honey?

00:01:08.947 --> 00:01:10.499
Speak if you love honey.

00:01:10.499 --> 00:01:11.880
(dog barking)

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.713
(upbeat music)

00:01:13.713 --> 00:01:17.454
♪ You always make me feel like only you ♪

00:01:17.454 --> 00:01:18.570
Do you know that three quarters

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.765
of Americans don't get their
recommended fruit intake?

00:01:20.765 --> 00:01:21.823
That's a problem, folks.

00:01:21.823 --> 00:01:22.891
That is a problem.

00:01:22.891 --> 00:01:26.274
♪ The reason I'm going out of my mind ♪

00:01:26.274 --> 00:01:28.997
Tell me you would not eat that.

00:01:28.997 --> 00:01:30.146
Tell me you wouldn't eat that!

00:01:30.146 --> 00:01:32.396
I'm about to eat it myself.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.542
That's so good.

00:01:34.542 --> 00:01:35.553
It pops.

00:01:35.553 --> 00:01:38.626
(upbeat music)

00:01:38.626 --> 00:01:39.726
All right, I gotta go to work.

00:01:39.726 --> 00:01:43.027
Working weekends is not always
the most pleasant thing,

00:01:43.027 --> 00:01:44.950
but I think you kind of get used to it.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.537
Unmentioned benefit of working
on a weekend, no traffic.

00:01:49.537 --> 00:01:52.222
Even parking is easier on a weekend.

00:01:52.222 --> 00:01:53.938
Look how empty the parking lot is.

00:01:53.938 --> 00:01:54.771
Good morning, Ginny!

00:01:54.771 --> 00:01:56.104
- Oh, good morning, Mike.

00:01:56.104 --> 00:01:57.868
- Are these flowers for me? Be honest.

00:01:57.868 --> 00:01:59.241
- Oh, yes, they are.

00:01:59.241 --> 00:02:01.221
- You're lying.
- Beautiful tulips, yeah.

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:02.214
- Whose flowers are these?

00:02:02.214 --> 00:02:03.778
- They're gorgeous, aren't they?

00:02:03.778 --> 00:02:05.703
- Who gave them to you?
- Somebody.

00:02:05.703 --> 00:02:06.664
- Are we seeing patients?

00:02:06.664 --> 00:02:07.997
Are we saving lives, Ginny?

00:02:07.997 --> 00:02:09.681
- Yes we are!
- Okay, yay!

00:02:09.681 --> 00:02:10.898
- Today it's interesting in that

00:02:10.898 --> 00:02:12.605
there's a really good
balance of virtual visits

00:02:12.605 --> 00:02:14.189
and in-person appointments.

00:02:14.189 --> 00:02:15.672
And I just did a virtual appointment

00:02:15.672 --> 00:02:18.623
with a patient who I'm
checking in with regarding

00:02:18.623 --> 00:02:19.456
their mental health.

00:02:19.456 --> 00:02:22.111
We've been working on
treating their depression,

00:02:22.111 --> 00:02:25.616
their anxiety, pain issues
that were going on as a result

00:02:25.616 --> 00:02:27.460
of several accidents in their life.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:28.689
The beauty of family medicine is

00:02:28.689 --> 00:02:29.909
that we can think holistically.

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:32.628
So we look at the whole
picture, especially as a D.O.

00:02:32.628 --> 00:02:34.649
I just want to show you
guys something cool here.

00:02:34.649 --> 00:02:36.973
Here's our lab where
we do some of our work.

00:02:36.973 --> 00:02:39.186
We have the microscopy here

00:02:39.186 --> 00:02:40.984
if we're doing any kind of slides.

00:02:40.984 --> 00:02:43.144
Here we have the urine dipstick test.

00:02:43.144 --> 00:02:45.182
We have our strep tests,
our pregnancy tests.

00:02:45.182 --> 00:02:46.499
Basically what happens in this room

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:47.773
is point-of-care testing.

00:02:47.773 --> 00:02:49.543
Anything that you need
to do that can be done

00:02:49.543 --> 00:02:51.155
in office happens in that room.

00:02:51.155 --> 00:02:53.045
We're delivering those
results instantaneously

00:02:53.045 --> 00:02:54.647
to the patient, that will allow us to make

00:02:54.647 --> 00:02:56.241
on the spot clinical decisions.

00:02:56.241 --> 00:02:58.475
A lot of times you don't
need a lab test in order

00:02:58.475 --> 00:03:01.181
to get a diagnosis, but sometimes
it really helps facilitate

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:03.528
that process and rule conditions out.

00:03:03.528 --> 00:03:05.609
Something else I want to
share with you is that

00:03:05.609 --> 00:03:06.442
because we're family medicine,

00:03:06.442 --> 00:03:08.215
we take care of pediatric patients

00:03:08.215 --> 00:03:10.631
and we understand the
importance of development

00:03:10.631 --> 00:03:13.001
and learning how to read and consistently

00:03:13.001 --> 00:03:14.195
challenging our brains.

00:03:14.195 --> 00:03:16.348
We actually have a
Reach Out &amp; Read program

00:03:16.348 --> 00:03:19.442
where we stock like
huge sums of books here.

00:03:19.442 --> 00:03:21.378
And basically what we try and do is

00:03:21.378 --> 00:03:23.505
in every pediatric appointment
is to get a child set up

00:03:23.505 --> 00:03:26.851
with at least one new book
so they can continue reading,

00:03:26.851 --> 00:03:29.651
continue exploring, learning
about medical subjects.

00:03:29.651 --> 00:03:31.025
And the beauty is we have them

00:03:31.025 --> 00:03:32.959
in multiple languages as well.

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:35.789
I want to introduce you to my best friend,

00:03:35.789 --> 00:03:37.493
the interpretation cart.

00:03:37.493 --> 00:03:39.416
While I speak okay Spanish,

00:03:39.416 --> 00:03:41.924
when you get a complaint
like fatigue, nausea,

00:03:41.924 --> 00:03:44.415
these really non-specific symptoms,

00:03:44.415 --> 00:03:47.252
meaning they don't tie
directly to an illness,

00:03:47.252 --> 00:03:48.738
you have to ask a lot of questions.

00:03:48.738 --> 00:03:49.955
You have to get a good understanding

00:03:49.955 --> 00:03:51.867
of what the patient is describing.

00:03:51.867 --> 00:03:54.114
The history is probably
the most important aspect.

00:03:54.114 --> 00:03:56.523
Having an in-person translator like this

00:03:56.523 --> 00:03:58.078
where they can visualize the patient,

00:03:58.078 --> 00:03:59.823
the patient can visualize the interpreter.

00:03:59.823 --> 00:04:02.812
It goes such a long way to
helping diagnose patients.

00:04:02.812 --> 00:04:04.338
It improves quality of care.

00:04:04.338 --> 00:04:06.071
This is where tech comes in handy.

00:04:06.071 --> 00:04:08.630
This is how we improve the
doctor-patient relationship

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:09.657
instead of harming it,

00:04:09.657 --> 00:04:11.097
because the doctors that sit there

00:04:11.097 --> 00:04:12.007
in the middle of patient visits

00:04:12.007 --> 00:04:13.228
that just type the whole time,

00:04:13.228 --> 00:04:14.862
I think that hurts more than it helps.

00:04:14.862 --> 00:04:16.959
Ginny, can you imagine how long
we've been working together?

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.047
Are we going to celebrate our
10 year anniversary, Ginny?

00:04:19.047 --> 00:04:21.312
- Sure, with a bottle of champagne.

00:04:21.312 --> 00:04:22.559
- Do you have fun working with me?

00:04:22.559 --> 00:04:24.232
- Yes, you're like a little kid, yes!

00:04:24.232 --> 00:04:25.315
- But that's not good!

00:04:25.315 --> 00:04:26.594
You don't want a doctor
who's a little kid.

00:04:26.594 --> 00:04:27.427
What's up?

00:04:28.318 --> 00:04:30.254
I heard you might watch a video or two?

00:04:30.254 --> 00:04:32.634
- I watch a lot of videos.
- Really?

00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:34.689
Be honest, is this guy a good boyfriend?

00:04:34.689 --> 00:04:36.403
- He's amazing.
- Yeah?

00:04:36.403 --> 00:04:37.431
Well, we love him here,

00:04:37.431 --> 00:04:39.453
so we hope you love him just as much.

00:04:39.453 --> 00:04:41.007
- I do, I love him.

00:04:41.007 --> 00:04:42.575
- Aww, nice meeting you.

00:04:42.575 --> 00:04:43.437
Good work, good work.

00:04:43.437 --> 00:04:44.534
- Thank you so much. Bye!

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:46.630
- You get a YouTube
shout out, are you hyped?

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:47.761
You got hyped?

00:04:47.761 --> 00:04:49.775
- Very very hyped. I love you, Mike.

00:04:49.775 --> 00:04:50.856
- I love you, bro.

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:53.365
This is how you know, we're
passionate about our health.

00:04:53.365 --> 00:04:57.115
Look at that pull up
bar right in the office.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:04.834
Guess who's done with their shift

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:07.259
and gets to go home to Bear? This guy.

00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:09.723
All right, I just pulled up
to my place, get to park up

00:05:09.723 --> 00:05:11.443
have a little midday snack.

00:05:11.443 --> 00:05:12.754
And you know these navel oranges

00:05:12.754 --> 00:05:14.246
actually contain dietary fiber

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:15.967
which I love because we, first of all

00:05:15.967 --> 00:05:17.725
under eat fiber, generally speaking.

00:05:17.725 --> 00:05:20.446
Dietary fiber can help control
appetite, decrease rates

00:05:20.446 --> 00:05:25.115
of type two diabetes, obesity,
heart disease, et cetera.

00:05:25.115 --> 00:05:26.632
That's my midday snack right there.

00:05:26.632 --> 00:05:27.626
And when I arrive home

00:05:27.626 --> 00:05:30.226
the first person to greet
me is the Newfoundland.

00:05:30.226 --> 00:05:32.559
Newfoundland! Floofy floofy.

00:05:34.825 --> 00:05:37.041
All right, I think it's
time to take out the newf.

00:05:37.041 --> 00:05:38.374
Newf, you ready?

00:05:40.152 --> 00:05:41.270
He doesn't look ready.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.078
I like to train him a little
bit before I head outside.

00:05:44.078 --> 00:05:47.840
I feel like if I do a
few exercises with him to

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.728
re-instill his training,
he does better on walks.

00:05:50.728 --> 00:05:53.935
Lately this guy's been slacking.

00:05:53.935 --> 00:05:55.484
Touch.

00:05:55.484 --> 00:05:56.850
Good boy. Sit.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:01.350
The idea here is that he
waits no matter how far I go.

00:06:02.984 --> 00:06:03.901
Okay, come.

00:06:05.226 --> 00:06:06.467
Good boy!

00:06:06.467 --> 00:06:09.295
The only struggle of
giving this guy treats?

00:06:09.295 --> 00:06:12.002
Ew, you see the drool? Look at the drool!

00:06:12.002 --> 00:06:13.120
Sit.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:13.953
Oh, what a handsome fella.

00:06:13.953 --> 00:06:15.308
Can I have a paw?

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:16.141
Good boy.

00:06:16.141 --> 00:06:16.974
Nice to meet you.

00:06:16.974 --> 00:06:18.692
Can I have the other paw?

00:06:18.692 --> 00:06:19.525
Speak.

00:06:19.525 --> 00:06:20.402
(dog barks)

00:06:20.402 --> 00:06:21.235
Good boy.

00:06:21.235 --> 00:06:22.152
Okay. Down.

00:06:24.111 --> 00:06:24.944
Play dead!

00:06:25.878 --> 00:06:27.225
(laughs)

00:06:27.225 --> 00:06:29.969
Okay Bear, come on! Yeah!

00:06:29.969 --> 00:06:31.596
So right now I'm going
to head over to the bank.

00:06:31.596 --> 00:06:32.929
Do some errands.

00:06:33.804 --> 00:06:35.209
He doesn't even run.

00:06:35.209 --> 00:06:36.100
He's just hops around.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.710
Bear, stop peeing on everything.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.884
It's such a gorgeous day
out in New York City. Wow.

00:06:39.884 --> 00:06:42.449
Look how good Bear is
while I'm at the ATM.

00:06:42.449 --> 00:06:44.527
Just waiting for me, being a good boy.

00:06:44.527 --> 00:06:46.773
One of the funniest parts
about walking with Bear is

00:06:46.773 --> 00:06:48.648
that everyone in New York
City likes to scream,

00:06:48.648 --> 00:06:50.333
Oh my God, look at that bear!

00:06:50.333 --> 00:06:52.780
and I'm like, little did
you know, his name is Bear.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.951
After walking Bear, I just
hopped in a quick shower.

00:06:54.951 --> 00:06:58.703
I'm about to do a live stream
with an awesome university.

00:06:58.703 --> 00:07:02.281
Talk with their pre-med
pre-health society here.

00:07:02.281 --> 00:07:03.394
I'm really excited.

00:07:03.394 --> 00:07:06.323
My setup is kind of really
messy here, but that's okay.

00:07:06.323 --> 00:07:08.509
As long as the background
looks great, let's do it.

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:09.342
All right.

00:07:09.342 --> 00:07:10.705
Late night sessions in the gym.

00:07:10.705 --> 00:07:11.785
I'm hitting back today.

00:07:11.785 --> 00:07:12.960
I'm about to do some lat pull downs.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.210
Here. You can come watch this exercise.

00:07:23.508 --> 00:07:24.562
Good set, high five.

00:07:24.562 --> 00:07:26.988
Now it's time to hit the lower
lats, a little rear deltoid.

00:07:26.988 --> 00:07:29.571
I'm going to do some low roads.

00:07:34.636 --> 00:07:37.653
The key here is to not rush your reps.

00:07:37.653 --> 00:07:40.784
Post gym, having a little OJ here.

00:07:40.784 --> 00:07:41.853
Fresh squeeze action.

00:07:41.853 --> 00:07:43.651
It's a full orange day
for me, as you can tell.

00:07:43.651 --> 00:07:46.191
And actually juice,
post-workout; not a bad idea.

00:07:46.191 --> 00:07:48.533
High carb load equals insulin kick.

00:07:48.533 --> 00:07:51.365
It actually pushes the nutrients
back into your muscles.

00:07:51.365 --> 00:07:52.415
After a hard workout

00:07:52.415 --> 00:07:54.844
I need a little replenishment
of my glycogen stores.

00:07:54.844 --> 00:07:55.677
So cheers.

00:07:55.677 --> 00:07:58.034
Bear, what do you think bud?

00:07:58.034 --> 00:08:00.617
(upbeat music)

